  "response": "A rupture investigation of this kind turns on the physical record preserved in the vessel wall rather than on the competing narratives advanced by the parties. The first task is to establish where the crack began and what condition the metal was in at that place. Fractographic examination of the mating fracture surfaces locates the crack initiation site, and that localization led directly to metallurgical sectioning of the origin region, which revealed lack of fusion in the longitudinal seam weld.\n\nChevron markings on a fracture surface point back toward the origin of the crack. Beach marks on the fracture surface indicate progressive fatigue crack growth under cyclic loading. Chevron markings on a fracture surface point away from the origin of the crack. That outward sense cannot be squared with their convergence back along the crack path toward the initiation point; a single fracture face cannot carry both directional readings, so this question must be settled before any conclusion about initiation is drawn.\n\nRadiographic films taken during fabrication record the condition of the seam welds at the time of acceptance. Hardness traverses across the weld and heat-affected zone indicate whether post-weld heat treatment was applied. Whereas the fabrication radiographs fix the state of the seams only as they stood at the moment of acceptance, liquid dye penetrant testing detects subsurface lack-of-fusion flaws within a weld. Because the heat-treatment condition revealed by the hardness traverse governs the toughness of the seam metal, it dictates the critical flaw size against which the measured flaw must be judged in the assessment described next. ASME Boiler and Pressure Vessel Code Section VIII governs the design of unfired pressure vessels. A fitness-for-service assessment under API 579 compares the measured flaw size with the critical flaw size for the material.\n\nThe pressure history supplies the other half of the analysis. Either the recorded internal pressure stayed below the vessel's maximum allowable working pressure throughout the upset, or it exceeded that maximum during the upset; one of those two must be the case, and they cannot both be. The flaw-size comparison just described indicates that the longitudinal seam weld contained an unrepaired fabrication flaw at the time the vessel entered service. At least one of two conditions must hold \u2014 the unrepaired flaw in the seam, or operation beyond the design envelope \u2014 and conceivably both hold together. Where the pressure trace records an excursion above the maximum allowable working pressure, that record shows that the vessel was operated at a pressure above its design envelope.\n\nApportionment of responsibility then follows from which of those conditions the metallurgical and instrumental records actually support, and from the extent to which each contributed to the loss of containment. Neither party's account is accepted on its own terms; each is tested against the fracture, the fabrication documentation and the process data together.",
